{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "צו את אהרן", :translit "tzav et aharon", :gloss "Command Aaron"} {:he "לאשמה בה", :translit "le'ashmah bah", :gloss "for it is a guilt-offering"} {:he "ששגגת תלמוד עולה זדון", :translit "she-shig'gat talmud oleh zedon", :gloss "for oversight in study is accounted as intentional"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "He notes the last words of the preceding verse (Lev 5:19) read לאשמה בה, linking it to the command to Aaron."}], :frame {:text "The principle that neglect of Torah study is treated as intentional is found at Shabbat 138b and is stated explicitly by Resh Lakish: one who forgets a single thing of his learning transgresses a negative commandment. The term זריזין, alacrity in mitzvot, is a standard rabbinic value (Pesahim 3a, Shabbat 4a).", :sources ["Shabbat 138b" "Pesahim 3a" "Shabbat 4a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "משה", :translit "moshe", :gloss "Moses"} {:he "לאמר צו את", :translit "le'emor tzav et", :gloss "saying: command"} {:he "תורה צוה", :translit "torah tzivah", :gloss "He commanded Torah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "לאמר צו את", :spells "תורה צוה"}], :frame {:text "The verse itself states in its opening phrase, 'Command Aaron and his sons, saying' (Leviticus 6:2). The tradition that Torah study is equivalent to sacrifice is found at Menahot 110a, where the phrase 'This is the law of' is interpreted to mean that whoever engages in the law of a given offering is regarded as though he offered it.", :sources ["Leviticus 6:2" "Menahot 110a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "זאת תורת העול", :translit "zot torat ha'olah", :gloss "This is the law of the burnt-offering"} {:he "עו\"לה", :translit "'olah", :gloss "burnt-offering (abbreviation)"} {:he "אשה", :translit "ishsheh", :gloss "fire-offering"} {:he "אש", :translit "esh", :gloss "fire"} {:he "הלא כה דברי כאש", :translit "halo koh devarai ke'esh", :gloss "Is not My word thus like fire"} {:he "מימינו אש דת למו", :translit "miyemino esh dat lamo", :gloss "from His right hand, a fiery law for them"} {:he "תלמוד", :translit "talmud", :gloss "study"} {:he "את קרבני לחמי", :translit "et korbani lachmi", :gloss "the bread of My offering"} {:he "לכו לחמי בלחמי", :translit "lechu lachmi belachmi", :gloss "Come, partake of My bread"} {:he "כשם שאי אפשר לעולם בלא לחם כך א\"א לעולם בלא תורה", :translit "k'shem she'e efshar la'olam belo lechem kach e efshar la'olam belo torah", :gloss "Just as the world cannot exist without bread, so the world cannot exist without Torah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Rearranging the letters of אשה yields אש; likewise rearranging לחמי yields תלמוד."} {:kind :other, :note "The ה at the end of העול hints at the word עולה."}], :frame {:text "At Menahot 110a, the Sages derive from verses beginning 'This is the law of' that whoever engages in Torah study of a given offering is regarded as though he offered it. Sacrifices are called אשה (fire-offering) throughout Leviticus (e.g., Leviticus 1:9). The verse 'Is not My word thus like fire?' (Jeremiah 23:29) and 'from His right hand, a fiery law for them' (Deuteronomy 33:2) establish that Torah is called fire. Sacrifices are called לחם in Leviticus 3:11 and 3:16, 'the bread of My offering.' Torah is called לחם in Proverbs 9:5, 'Come, partake of my bread.' The statement that the world cannot exist without Torah is found at the conclusion of Shabbat 138b and in parallels such as Pirkei DeRabbi Eliezer.", :sources ["Menahot 110a" "Leviticus 1:9" "Jeremiah 23:29" "Deuteronomy 33:2" "Leviticus 3:11" "Proverbs 9:5" "Shabbat 138b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "He notes the last words of the preceding verse (Lev 5:19) read לאשמה בה, linking it to the command to Aaron.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :sofei-tevot, :phrase "לאמר צו את", :spells "תורה צוה", :letters ["ר" "ו" "ת"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :other, :note "Rearranging the letters of אשה yields אש; likewise rearranging לחמי yields תלמוד.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind 